  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-md-1",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "# C# in Arima Notebooks\n\nArima supports **C#** cells via `dotnet-script` — the .NET scripting tool.\n\n## Setup (one-time)\n1. Install the [.NET SDK](https://dot.net) (free, Windows / macOS / Linux)\n2. Install dotnet-script: `dotnet tool install -g dotnet-script`\n3. Restart Arima\n\n## Cell mode\nClick the mode button on any code cell to cycle through: **JShell → Java → JS → C# → F#**\n\n## Built-in helpers\n```csharp\nBaristaHtml(\"<b>bold</b>\");   // render HTML in output\nBaristaDisplay(myObject);      // Console.WriteLine shorthand\nBaristaTable(myList);          // ASCII table for any IEnumerable\n```\n\n> Install NuGet packages in the **Packages → NuGet** tab — they are auto-injected into every C# cell.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-1",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "csharp",
      "source": "//@ anchor: hello-world-in-c\n//@ description: Hello World in C#\n// A C# cell is a script, not a program file: dotnet-script wraps what you write\n// in a top-level entry point, so there is no class and no Main to declare.\nConsole.WriteLine(\"Hello from Arima Notebooks!\");\n\n// $\"...\" is an interpolated string. Everything inside the braces is real C#,\n// evaluated and then converted to text. Because it is compiled rather than\n// substituted, a misspelled name here is a build error, not a bad line of output.\nConsole.WriteLine($\".NET version: {Environment.Version}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"OS: {Environment.OSVersion}\");",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "hello-world-in-c",
      "description": "Hello World in C#"
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-md-2",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## Variables and Types\n\nC# is statically typed. Use `var` for type inference.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-2",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "csharp",
      "source": "//@ anchor: value-types-strings-and-interpolation\n//@ description: Value types, strings, and interpolation\n// `var` is not \"dynamic\". The compiler reads the initializer, infers one fixed\n// type, and pins it there for good: name is a string forever, version a double.\n// You are saving keystrokes, not giving up type checking.\nvar name    = \"Arima\";\nvar version = 1.0;       // a bare literal with a decimal point is double, not float\n\n// Naming the type explicitly is equivalent. Prefer it when the initializer does\n// not make the type obvious to someone reading the line.\nint year       = DateTime.Now.Year;\nbool isAwesome = true;   // a bool renders as \"True\"/\"False\", capitalised\n\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Project: {name} v{version}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Year: {year}, Awesome: {isAwesome}\");\n\n// C# strings are immutable. Trim() and ToUpper() do not edit `sentence`; each\n// hands back a brand-new string. That is why they chain safely - and why the\n// second line below has to call Trim() again to measure the trimmed length.\nstring sentence = \"  Hello, World!  \";\nConsole.WriteLine(sentence.Trim().ToUpper());\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Length: {sentence.Trim().Length}\");",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "value-types-strings-and-interpolation",
      "description": "Value types, strings, and interpolation"
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-md-3",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## Collections and LINQ\n\n`System.Linq` is included automatically — use it for powerful data queries.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-3",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "csharp",
      "source": "//@ anchor: list-and-linq\n//@ description: List and LINQ\n// List<int> is a growable array. The braces are a collection initializer -\n// shorthand for constructing the list and calling Add once per element.\nvar scores = new List<int> { 85, 92, 78, 95, 88, 73, 96, 81, 69, 90 };\n\n// `s => s >= 75` is a lambda: \"given an s, say whether it is at least 75\".\n// Where does not run it here. LINQ operators are deferred - they build a\n// description of the query and touch the data only when someone asks for a\n// result. ToList() is that ask: it forces the work and snapshots the answer.\nvar passing = scores.Where(s => s >= 75).ToList();\n\n// Average cannot be deferred - it has to see every element before it can name\n// a number - so it runs immediately and returns a double.\nvar avg = scores.Average();\n\n// Operators compose into a chain. Take(3) stops pulling once it has three\n// items; the sort in front of it still has to look at everything, because you\n// cannot know the largest value without inspecting them all.\nvar top3 = scores.OrderByDescending(s => s).Take(3).ToList();\n\nConsole.WriteLine($\"All scores: {string.Join(\", \", scores)}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Passing (>=75): {string.Join(\", \", passing)}\");\n\n// The text after the colon is a format specifier applied to the value, not part\n// of it: F1 means fixed-point with one decimal place.\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Average: {avg:F1}\");\nConsole.WriteLine($\"Top 3: {string.Join(\", \", top3)}\");",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "list-and-linq",
      "description": "List and LINQ"
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-md-4",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## Classes and Records\n\nC# supports full OOP. Use `record` for immutable data types.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-4",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "csharp",
      "source": "//@ anchor: record-types-c-9-perfect-for-data\n//@ description: Record types (C# 9+) - perfect for data\n// One line declares a whole immutable data type. The compiler writes the\n// constructor, the read-only properties, a readable ToString(), and - the part\n// that changes how you use it - *value* equality: two Products are Equal when\n// every field matches. A plain class would instead compare object identity, so\n// two separately-built Laptops would count as different things.\nrecord Product(string Name, string Category, decimal Price, int Stock);\n\n// `new(...)` with no type name is a target-typed new: the element type is\n// already known to be Product, so repeating it would only add noise.\n// The `m` suffix makes each price a decimal. Use decimal rather than double for\n// money: decimal is base 10, so amounts like 999.99 are stored exactly and\n// cents never drift during arithmetic.\nvar inventory = new List<Product> {\n    new(\"Laptop\",    \"Electronics\", 999.99m,  15),\n    new(\"Notebook\",  \"Stationery\",    3.49m, 200),\n    new(\"Monitor\",   \"Electronics\", 349.00m,  22),\n    new(\"Pen Pack\",  \"Stationery\",    8.99m, 150),\n    new(\"Keyboard\",  \"Electronics\",  79.99m,  40),\n};\n\n// Each operator returns a new sequence rather than reordering the source, so\n// `inventory` is exactly as it was after this query - important, because the\n// last line below reads it again.\nvar electronics = inventory\n    .Where(p => p.Category == \"Electronics\")\n    .OrderByDescending(p => p.Price)\n    .ToList();\n\nConsole.WriteLine(\"Electronics (by price):\");\nforeach (var p in electronics)\n    // The number after the comma is a field width: -12 pads to 12 columns and\n    // left-aligns (negative means left), 8 right-aligns in 8 columns, and :F2\n    // then fixes the decimals. Fixed widths are what turn a loop of WriteLine\n    // calls into something that reads as a table.\n    Console.WriteLine($\"  {p.Name,-12} ${p.Price,8:F2}  (stock: {p.Stock})\");\n\n// Sum takes a lambda that says *what* to add up - here value on hand per row,\n// summed across the inventory. :N2 adds thousands separators.\nConsole.WriteLine($\"\\nTotal inventory value: ${inventory.Sum(p => p.Price * p.Stock):N2}\");",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "record-types-c-9-perfect-for-data",
      "description": "Record types (C# 9+) — perfect for data"
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-md-5",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## BaristaTable Helper\n\nUse `BaristaTable()` to render any `IEnumerable` as an ASCII table.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-5",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "csharp",
      "source": "//@ anchor: employee\n//@ description: Defines Employee type\nrecord Employee(string Name, string Dept, int YearsExp, decimal Salary);\n\nvar team = new List<Employee> {\n    new(\"Alice\",  \"Engineering\", 5, 95000m),\n    new(\"Bob\",    \"Design\",      3, 72000m),\n    new(\"Carol\",  \"Engineering\", 8, 120000m),\n    new(\"Dave\",   \"Marketing\",   2, 65000m),\n    new(\"Eve\",    \"Engineering\", 4, 88000m),\n};\n\n// BaristaTable takes any IEnumerable and reflects over the element type: the\n// property names become the column headers, each element becomes a row. Records\n// pair well with it precisely because their properties are declared up front.\nBaristaTable(team);\n\n// GroupBy turns a flat list into a sequence of groups. Each group is itself an\n// IEnumerable of the employees that matched, and carries the value that formed\n// it as .Key - here, the department name.\nvar avgByDept = team\n    .GroupBy(e => e.Dept)\n    // `new { ... }` builds an anonymous type: a throwaway class the compiler\n    // generates from these property names. It is the usual way to reshape a\n    // group into a summary row you only need inside this one query.\n    .Select(g => new { Dept = g.Key, Count = g.Count(), AvgSalary = g.Average(e => e.Salary) })\n    .OrderBy(x => x.Dept);\n\n// Note the missing ToList(): avgByDept is still an unexecuted query. It runs\n// below, when foreach starts pulling from it. Enumerate it twice and the\n// grouping and averaging happen twice - which is when you reach for ToList().\nConsole.WriteLine(\"\\nAverage salary by department:\");\nforeach (var row in avgByDept)\n    Console.WriteLine($\"  {row.Dept,-15} {row.Count} engineers  avg: ${row.AvgSalary:N0}\");",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false,
      "anchor": "employee",
      "description": "Defines Employee type"
    },
    {
      "id": "cs-intro-md-6",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## Pipeline Annotations\n\nC# cells support the same `//@ anchor:` and `//@ depends:` annotations as JShell cells.\n\n```csharp\n//@ anchor: loadData\n// ... load data ...\n```\n\n```csharp\n//@ depends: loadData\n// runs after loadData\n```\n\nSee **csharp-201** for a full pipeline walkthrough.",
      "output": "",
      "executed": false
    }
  ],
